<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  SPEC.md 4.4a - Unrelenting Force throws targets, it does not teleport them.

  PawnFlyer is the vanilla mechanism behind skip and jump: the pawn is lifted, arcs through the
  air over real time, and lands. PawnFlyerBase lives in CORE (Data\Core\Defs\ThingDefs_Misc\
  Ethereal_Various.xml) - only the PawnJumper subclass is DLC-gated - so this works on the
  baseline environment with no DLC at all.

  Fast and flat compared to a jump: this is being hit by a wall of sound, not leaping.
-->
<Defs>

  <ThingDef ParentName="PawnFlyerBase">
    <defName>Dovahkiin_ShoutFlyer</defName>
    <label>thrown</label>
    <!--
      thingClass MUST be PawnJumper, not the base PawnFlyer.

      PawnFlyer does not override DrawAt or DrawPos - only PawnJumper does. A raw PawnFlyer
      therefore falls through to Thing.DrawAt, tries to draw its own graphic, finds none
      (PawnFlyerBase has no graphicData), and renders RimWorld's missing-texture marker.
      That is precisely the "red crossed square" seen in playtest while the pawn was in flight.
      PawnJumper draws the carried pawn and its shadow correctly.

      The PawnJumper *ThingDef* is DLC-gated, but the CLASS lives in Assembly-CSharp and is
      always available - so this works on the baseline environment with no DLC.
    -->
    <thingClass>PawnJumper</thingClass>
    <pawnFlyer>
      <!--
        Speed is matched to the shout wave's propagation (Thing_ShoutWave, 10 cells/second) so
        the victim travels with the blast rather than ahead of or behind it.
        Playtest 1 used speed 22, which crossed seven tiles in a third of a second and read as
        a teleport.
      -->
      <flightDurationMin>0.45</flightDurationMin>
      <flightSpeed>10</flightSpeed>
    </pawnFlyer>
  </ThingDef>

  <!-- Carrier for the travelling shout cone. Ethereal: never drawn, never selectable,
       never blocks anything. It ticks only while a shout is in flight, then vanishes. -->
  <ThingDef ParentName="EtherealThingBase">
    <defName>Dovahkiin_ShoutWave</defName>
    <label>shout</label>
    <thingClass>Dovahkiin.Thing_ShoutWave</thingClass>
    <tickerType>Normal</tickerType>
    <useHitPoints>false</useHitPoints>
    <drawerType>None</drawerType>
    <altitudeLayer>Skyfaller</altitudeLayer>
  </ThingDef>

  <!--
    SPEC.md 4.4e - Storm Call. The storm itself, carrying the strike schedule.

    Same shape as the shout wave: ethereal, never drawn, never selectable, ticks only while
    the shout is running and then vanishes. The lightning is drawn by vanilla's own
    WeatherEvent_LightningStrike, so this Thing has nothing to render.
  -->
  <ThingDef ParentName="EtherealThingBase">
    <defName>Dovahkiin_StormCall</defName>
    <label>storm</label>
    <thingClass>Dovahkiin.Thing_StormCall</thingClass>
    <tickerType>Normal</tickerType>
    <useHitPoints>false</useHitPoints>
    <drawerType>None</drawerType>
    <altitudeLayer>Skyfaller</altitudeLayer>
  </ThingDef>

  <!--
    SPEC.md 4.4d - the Dragon Aspect overlay.

    Unlike the two above, this one DOES draw, so it is the only ethereal Thing here with
    drawerType RealtimeOnly rather than None: Thing.DrawAt is called only for a Thing whose
    drawer runs in real time.

    It rides the PAWN rather than its own cell - DrawAt ignores the position it is handed and
    reads pawn.Drawer.DrawPos instead - so it follows movement, facing, drafting and downing
    without touching PawnRenderer, which is what RocketMan would fight. See the file header of
    Thing_DragonAspectOverlay.cs for the two routes that were rejected and why.

    tickerType Rare because it only has to notice that the hediff has ended, which is not a
    per-tick question. It holds no game state: destroying it loses the picture and nothing else.

    drawOffscreen MATTERS and is not decoration. RimWorld culls dynamic drawing by the THING's
    own cell, and this Thing never moves from where it was cast - it draws at the pawn instead.
    Without this flag the armour silently stopped rendering as soon as the pawn walked far
    enough from the spot they shouted on, while the hediff carried on running, so the buff
    looked like it had worn off hours early. Vanilla's other RealtimeOnly movers, Tornado and
    PawnFlyerBase, both set it for the same reason.
  -->
  <ThingDef ParentName="EtherealThingBase">
    <defName>Dovahkiin_DragonAspectOverlay</defName>
    <label>dragon aspect</label>
    <thingClass>Dovahkiin.Thing_DragonAspectOverlay</thingClass>
    <tickerType>Rare</tickerType>
    <useHitPoints>false</useHitPoints>
    <drawerType>RealtimeOnly</drawerType>
    <drawOffscreen>true</drawOffscreen>
    <altitudeLayer>PawnState</altitudeLayer>
  </ThingDef>

</Defs>
